WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.780
- One question we get asked a lot

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.010
is whether you should
use commercial outlines

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.290
or commercial supplements in law school.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.790
Today we're going to
tackle the good and the bad

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.361
of commercial outlines.

00:00:10.361 --> 00:00:13.528
(lighthearted jingle)

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.740
In general we love commercial supplements

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.760
and commercial outlines

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.708
and highly recommend them in law school,

00:00:20.708 --> 00:00:23.780
but not all supplements are created equal.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.270
Let's go over the good and the bad.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.560
We'll start with the good.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.580
First, commercial supplements help explain

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.200
complex and complicated subjects.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.210
They are designed specifically
to provide clarity

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.770
where your professor is unclear.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.110
They can provide great summaries

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.000
of important parts of cases,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.950
especially when an outline is key

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.300
to your particular casebook,

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.520
where an outline goes
over each individual case

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.950
that you're likely to find
in the casebook itself.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.700
A commercial outline can
give you crucial insight

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.670
as to why certain cases are important

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.390
and what the important takeaways are

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.200
from that particular case.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.810
This is in fact the foundation
of our flipped case method,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.210
which we talk about in another
video linked down below.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.580
But commercial outlines are not perfect.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.750
Some of the downsides include

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.410
the fact that a commercial
outline might cover a subject

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.083
or a legal topic differently
than your professor,

00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:17.950
but you should know that if your professor

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.030
has a different
understanding of the subject

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.600
you should go with the understanding

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.040
that your professor has.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.450
Your professor trumps
the commercial outline.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.460
Their version will be on the test,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.660
not the supplement version.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.970
Another downside is that
a commercial outline

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.660
might confuse you.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.990
It's possible that getting
a different version

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.750
or explanation might take
something that you understand

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.440
and actually confuse you,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.290
so you have to be wary when
you're getting a whole bunch

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.060
of different explanations
about the same thing.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.280
You have to be able to synthesize
all of that information.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.350
But on the whole, commercial
outlines are totally worth it.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.930
Think of it like a pyramid.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.331
You have to have a solid
foundation of the basics

00:01:55.331 --> 00:01:57.838
so you can get to the higher level stuff.

00:01:57.838 --> 00:01:59.880
Think of it like a game of chess.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.730
You have to understand
the foundations first

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.630
before you can move on to
the higher level strategies.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.970
If in chess the foundation is how

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.000
every individual piece moves,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.480
you have to understand that
a knight moves in an L shape

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.260
and a pawn moves one square at a time,

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.181
with certain exceptions of course.

00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:19.950
You literally cannot understand the second

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.660
and third level strategy without
understanding those basics,

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.460
and a commercial outline provides that.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.110
They also help you understand
what you are reading.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.920
So many law students dive into huge cases

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.140
without knowing why
that case is important,

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.028
and as a result they get bogged down

00:02:35.028 --> 00:02:38.276
in verbose appellate opinions.

00:02:38.276 --> 00:02:40.920
This is why the average
reading rate in law school

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.260
is only 10 pages per hour.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.810
It's also why it takes so long

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.510
to complete your reading assignments.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.300
That's why we advocate for
the flipped case method

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.140
where you read the supplements first

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.130
before you get to your cases.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.420
Read your commercial outlines

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.112
and your upper classmen's
outlines or Westlaw case notes

00:02:58.112 --> 00:03:00.930
before starting the
reading of the case itself.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.020
This will make you read the case faster

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.740
and with far more understanding

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.870
and it's one of the reasons
that commercial outlines

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.610
are so important.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.800
You are not cheating

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.320
if you're using a commercial supplement.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.700
I hear this a lot.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.100
There is this notion that unless you do

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.840
everything on your own
the hardest way possible,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.060
you're somehow cheating in law school.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.760
Using a commercial outline
is cheating in the same way

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.262
that having a piano teacher is cheating.

00:03:26.262 --> 00:03:28.977
That is to say it's not cheating at all.

00:03:28.977 --> 00:03:31.950
You are also not a failure if you use them

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.520
to understand your cases.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.480
These cases are assigned in law school

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.170
and they're intentionally old,

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.490
they're intentionally antiquated,

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.220
and they are hard to understand

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.540
so use commercial outlines in a way

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.847
that will help you understand them

00:03:43.847 --> 00:03:46.280
and in a way that will help
you retain that information

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.770
so that you can use it on the final exam.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.610
What will also help you on your final exam

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.612
is our free guide to law school
down below in the free link,

